course: dictionary
lesson: 16022
title: A long, bitter quarrel
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A vendetta is a quarrel that goes on for a long time, and each side keeps trying to get revenge. Read the words around it to find the clues. «The two families had been in a vendetta for fifty years.» Fifty years is a very long time, and the families keep hurting each other, so a vendetta is a long, bitter quarrel.
explain: «The villain carried on a vendetta against the detective who sent him to prison.» The words carried on show that the anger did not stop. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? A quarrel that never ends would fit, so the meaning is right.
explain: Some vendettas are personal, which means they are aimed at one person the writer dislikes. «Ben's rival started a personal vendetta, hiding his football kit every week.» Hiding kit every week is unfair and mean, so the vendetta is a long campaign to annoy Ben.
fact: «Vendetta» means a long, bitter quarrel where people keep taking revenge.
fact: A vendetta can be between two families, or one person can carry on a vendetta against another.
fact: «Vendetta» comes from Italian, where it means revenge.
fact: Useful phrases: «a vendetta against», «carry on a vendetta», «a personal vendetta».
fact: The clues to look for are words like long, years, carried on and personal.
exercise: «The two families had been in a vendetta for fifty years.» What does vendetta mean? | *A long, bitter quarrel where each side keeps taking revenge | A fast race between two cars | A friendly meeting to share a meal | A sudden picnic in the park
exercise: «Ben's rival started a personal vendetta, hiding his football kit every week.» What does personal suggest? | It happens only once | It is a happy game for everyone | The whole town is taking part | *The anger is aimed at one person
exercise: Where did the word vendetta come from? | Latin, where it means sleep | English, where it means bread | French, where it means party | *Italian, where it means revenge
test: «Her uncle carried on a vendetta against the man who took his land.» What does this tell you? | *The anger is long and personal | The uncle and the man became friends | The uncle was on holiday | The land was given away on purpose
test: Which phrase uses vendetta correctly? | a vendetta of cake | vendetta the bus | happy vendetta picnic | *carry on a vendetta against
exam: «After years of quarrelling, the two families were still locked in a vendetta and would not forgive.» What does vendetta mean here? | A short holiday | A long, bitter quarrel with revenge | *A quick, friendly visit | A kind of dance
